From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: nobody <>
Date: Thu, 15 Oct 2026 18:42:04 +1000
Subject: [PATCH] perf: vectorized, threaded Laya head and batched
 multi-question encode

The Laya decision head (2 pre-LN MHA + ReLU FFN layers on CPU) ran per-token
scalar GEMVs on one core: 94-97% of /v1/decisions latency (409 ms for 28
tokens, 1.36 s for 91, on a Zen 4 + RX 7900 XTX box with the encoder on GPU).

- dot_f32 with 16 independent accumulators (vectorizes without -ffast-math)
- batched linears: threads split output rows, each row reused across tokens
- attention threaded across heads; FFN batched over tokens
- persistent worker pool (spawning threads per section cost more than the work)
- laya_decide_batch: every question of a request encoded in one llama_process
  (one sequence per question, chunked by n_seq_max / n_ubatch)
- LLAMA_LAYA_THREADS overrides the pool size; LLAMA_LAYA_TIMING=1 logs
  encode / head ms per request

Measured end-to-end through zerollama: 1 question x 5 options 899 -> 92 ms,
5 options x 4 questions 3.41 s -> 138 ms. Outputs match the scalar path within
4e-4 (probabilities, 4 dp) with identical choices.
